### What is a distributed monolith?

A system split into multiple services across the network but that cannot deploy, fail, or scale independently. It has the operational complexity of microservices with none of their benefits. You pay the tax of network calls, service discovery, and distributed tracing but get none of the independence.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

public class DistributedMonolithSymptoms {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Symptom 1: shared database");
        System.out.println("Symptom 2: shared business-logic library");
        System.out.println("Symptom 3: synchronous call chains");
        System.out.println("Test: can each service deploy, fail, and scale independently?");
        System.out.println("If NO --> distributed monolith");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Symptom 1: shared database
Symptom 2: shared business-logic library
Symptom 3: synchronous call chains
Test: can each service deploy, fail, and scale independently?
If NO --> distributed monolith
```

### How does a shared database create coupling?

When multiple services read and write the same tables, a schema change in one service's context breaks all other services that touch those tables. Every column rename, type change, or constraint addition requires coordinated simultaneous redeploys.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;

public class SharedDatabaseProblem {
    // WRONG: both AccountService and LoanService touch the same accounts table
    public void accountServiceQuery(JdbcTemplate jdbc, String id) {
        jdbc.queryForObject(
            "SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = ?", String.class, id);
    }

    public void loanServiceQuery(JdbcTemplate jdbc, String id) {
        jdbc.queryForObject(
            "SELECT balance FROM accounts WHERE id = ?", String.class, id);
    }
    // If AccountService renames 'balance' to 'available_balance',
    // LoanService breaks --> forced coordinated deploy
}
```

### How does a shared library create coupling?

A shared library with business logic (domain models, validation rules) forces every consumer to rebuild and redeploy when the library changes. Independent deployment is destroyed.

```xml
<!-- WRONG: shared domain model in a common library -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>com.bank</groupId>
    <artifactId>common-domain</artifactId>
    <version>2.3.0</version>
</dependency>
<!-- Changing Account.java in common-domain forces ALL 5 consumers
     to rebuild and redeploy. This is lockstep, not independent. -->
```

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

public class SharedLibraryProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: sharing domain code
        System.out.println("WRONG: com.bank.common.Account used by 5 services");
        System.out.println("Change Account.java --> rebuild + redeploy all 5");

        // CORRECT: share only contracts (OpenAPI spec, Avro schema)
        System.out.println("CORRECT: each service has its OWN Account class");
        System.out.println("Share API schema, not code");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: com.bank.common.Account used by 5 services
Change Account.java --> rebuild + redeploy all 5
CORRECT: each service has its OWN Account class
Share API schema, not code
```

### How do synchronous call chains create cascading failures?

When the gateway calls Transfer which calls Account which calls Fraud which calls Credit, latency is the sum of all hops and availability is the product. One slow service blocks threads and cascades failure backward.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

public class SyncChainProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        double[] availability = {0.99, 0.99, 0.99, 0.99};
        double combined = 1.0;
        for (double a : availability) combined *= a;
        System.out.printf("Individual availability: 99%% each%n");
        System.out.printf("Chain availability: %.2f%% (4 hops)%n", combined * 100);
        System.out.println("4% failure rate from the chain alone!");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Individual availability: 99% each
Chain availability: 96.06% (4 hops)
4% failure rate from the chain alone!
```

### How do you fix each symptom?

| Symptom | Fix |
|---|---|
| Shared database | Database-per-service; access only through APIs or events |
| Shared business library | Share contracts (API schemas, event schemas), not code |
| Synchronous chains | Prefer async events; add timeouts, circuit breakers, bulkheads to remaining sync calls |
| Temporal coupling | Backward-compatible API evolution; never break a contract |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.kafka.annotation.KafkaListener;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class FixedArchitecture {

    // CORRECT: database-per-service
    // LoanService does NOT touch AccountService's tables.
    // Instead it calls the API:
    public BigDecimal getBalanceViaApi(String accountId) {
        // return accountClient.getBalance(accountId);
        return new BigDecimal("150000.00"); // stub
    }

    // Or reacts to events:
    @KafkaListener(topics = "account.balance-changed")
    public void onBalanceChanged(String event) {
        System.out.println("Received balance-changed event: " + event);
        // Update local read-only view
    }
}
```

### When should you merge services back?

If you cannot cleanly separate data and deployment, the services were split along the wrong boundaries. The right fix may be to merge them back into a modular monolith rather than fighting the coupling with more infrastructure.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class MergeDecision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean canSeparateData = false;
        boolean canDeployIndependently = false;

        if (!canSeparateData || !canDeployIndependently) {
            System.out.println("Cannot separate data or deploy independently.");
            System.out.println("MERGE back into a modular monolith.");
            System.out.println("Wrong boundaries were chosen.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Cannot separate data or deploy independently.
MERGE back into a modular monolith.
Wrong boundaries were chosen.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The distributed monolith is usually the result of splitting prematurely before boundaries were understood.

```java
package com.bank.antipattern;

public class CommonMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: splitting for "microservices" without understanding boundaries
        System.out.println("WRONG: 5 services, 1 shared DB, 1 shared library");
        System.out.println("Every release requires coordinating 5 teams");

        // CORRECT: start as modular monolith, extract when proven
        System.out.println("CORRECT: modular monolith with enforced boundaries");
        System.out.println("Extract only when independent need is proven");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: 5 services, 1 shared DB, 1 shared library
Every release requires coordinating 5 teams
CORRECT: modular monolith with enforced boundaries
Extract only when independent need is proven
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The distributed monolith is the most common failure mode of a microservices migration.
- Start as a modular monolith and extract only when a real need arises.
- If every release requires coordinating multiple services, you have a distributed monolith.
- The mark of seniority is recognizing when to merge back, not just when to split.
- The litmus test: can this service deploy, fail, and scale independently? If not, it is not a microservice.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class IndependenceTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean canDeployAlone = false;
        boolean canFailAlone   = false;
        boolean canScaleAlone  = false;

        if (!canDeployAlone || !canFailAlone || !canScaleAlone) {
            System.out.println("FAIL: service is not independently deployable/failable/scalable");
            System.out.println("This is a distributed monolith, not a microservice.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
FAIL: service is not independently deployable/failable/scalable
This is a distributed monolith, not a microservice.
```
